;; Kubernetes on a Compute Module 5.
;;
;; The same feature:k3s that runs on the QEMU machine, on real silicon: four
;; A76 cores and up to 16GB, which is the first board here that k3s is a
;; reasonable thing to ask of rather than a demonstration.
;;
;; profile:standard rather than profile:raspberrypi5, because k3s states what
;; it needs of userspace as capabilities - systemd-init and glibc - and the
;; board's own profile is BusyBox init. That is the composition doing its job:
;; the wrong pairing does not build and then misbehave, it does not compose.
;;
;; Storage is the thing to get right on hardware. k3s writes to
;; /var/lib/rancher constantly, and an SD card will not survive it for long.
;; The CM5 carriers all have an M.2 slot on PCIe, so the data directory
;; belongs on NVMe: that is a runtime decision, not a configuration one, and
;; this image does not make it. Flash it, add the drive, mount it there.
;;
;; Why bother, when the appliance daemons are 200KB binaries reading shared
;; memory and the image is already the deployment unit: because a customer
;; who runs k3s at the edge manages fleets with tooling they already have,
;; and being a workload in it is worth more to them than being a better
;; process on a board.

(image cm5-io-k3s

  (compose
    target:cm5-io
    feature:cm5-module
    profile:standard
    feature:networking
    feature:k3s)

  ;; As with qemu-arm-k3s: a value has no "at least", so the size cannot be
  ;; asked for by the feature that needs it. 60MB of binary plus the images
  ;; it unpacks, plus room for the container store before NVMe is mounted.
  (override
    (value buildroot:BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT2_SIZE "4G"))

  (verified-against (buildroot "2025.02.16")))
